    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "Association between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors for diet-sensitive non-communicable diseases in sub-Saharan Africa: a systematic review and meta-analysis",
      "reason": "The title explicitly identifies the report as 'a systematic review and meta-analysis'."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "Association between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors for diet-sensitive non-communicable diseases in sub-Saharan Africa",
      "reason": "The title states the exposure (food insecurity), outcomes (key metabolic risk factors for diet-sensitive NCDs), and population/setting (sub-Saharan Africa)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "a systematic review and meta-analysis",
      "reason": "The title provides supplementary information on the analysis method, indicating that a meta-analysis was performed."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 95
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "In previous studies, food insecurity has been hypothesised to promote the prevalence of metabolic risk factors on the causal pathway to diet-sensitive non-communicable diseases (NCDs).",
      "reason": "The abstract briefly notes prior knowledge (hypothesised link between food insecurity and metabolic risk factors), but the retrieved evidence does not include an Introduction section describing the current state of knowledge and important uncertainties in detail."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "reliable data on the trend and patterns of key metabolic risk factors among the food insecure population in resource-limited settings, such as sub-Saharan African countries still lag far behind",
      "reason": "The need for the review is implied (data gaps in sub-Saharan Africa, food insecurity affecting over 257 million people), but this appears in the Discussion rather than a dedicated Introduction rationale, and the full Introduction is not present in the retrieved evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since this was the first meta-analysis (quantitative synthesis) regarding the epidemiology of metabolic risks among the food insecure population in sub-Saharan Africa; we tried to compare our findings to other surveys and reviews conducted outside of the sub-Saharan Africa",
      "reason": "The manuscript claims to be the first meta-analysis on this topic in sub-Saharan Africa and discusses prior reviews (e.g., Arenas et al., Beltran et al.) in the Discussion, but it does not explicitly cite a previous systematic review as a rationale for updating or replicating it in the Introduction."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a review of observational associations between food insecurity and metabolic risk factors, not a review of intervention effects, so no mechanism of action of an intervention is required."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review examines associations between an exposure (food insecurity) and outcomes, not a complex intervention or delivery context, so a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 70
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The main research question: What are the associations between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors for diet-sensitive NCDs in the sub-Saharan African population?",
      "reason": "Objectives are explicitly stated in the abstract (text_0: 'aimed to determine the associations between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors... and estimate the prevalence...') and a main research question is explicitly formulated using the PEO (Population, Exposure, Outcomes) framework in the methodology (Table 1, text_2)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The included studies were cross-sectional study designs, the data for the relationship between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors were measured and analysed cross-sectionally rather than longitudinally",
      "reason": "This is a review of observational associations (exposure-outcome), not intervention effects; a PICO comparison element does not apply. The authors appropriately used the PEO/PEO-T framework instead."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 92
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_2",
      "quote": "Only original and quantitative study design that are peer-reviewed cross- sectional studies conducted in sub-Saharan Africa by trained personnel, in any publication language ... Studies conducted among sub-Saharan African populations Participants of any age group and sex ... Studies reporting a measure of food insecurity or food insufficiency as an inde- pendent variable ... Studies reporting at least one of the key metabolic risk factors as a dependent variable",
      "reason": "Table 2 (Eligibility criteria per PEO-T) plus Table 1 (PEO framework) fully specify eligible designs, population, exposure, and outcomes; the search section confirms sub-Saharan Africa as setting."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_2",
      "quote": "Complete peer-reviewed original research studies, published between January 2015 and June 2019 | Studies published before January 2015",
      "reason": "Report characteristics are specified: publication year range, peer-reviewed original research, and no language restriction ('in any publication language'; 'No language restrictions were imposed')."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The studies concerned were excluded after three unsuccessful attempts to contact the author.",
      "reason": "The PRISMA flow diagram distinguishes exclusions such as 'Irrelevant outcome, unclear diagnosis criteria' from non-retrievable full texts (author never responded), but the eligibility criteria text does not explicitly distinguish exclusion for outcomes not measured versus results not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "we recognised the need to stratify the evidence of associations as reported by the studies into three strata of associations: Adverse Association, Nonsignificant Association, and Inconclusive Association ... Studies with a low risk of bias and outcomes deemed to be sufficiently homogenous; were selected for inclusion in the metaanalysis to compute the pooled prevalence estimates.",
      "reason": "Synthesis groups are clearly defined: narrative synthesis strata of association types and meta-analysis inclusion (sufficiently homogeneous, low risk of bias), linked to the review objectives of associations and prevalence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "However, the year of publication was limited to between January 2015 and October 2019 to ensure that the evidence of associations is demonstrably rooted in the most recent and up-to-date scientific literature. This decision was chiefly because practitioners and decision-makers are somewhat interested and encouraged to make use of the most current evidence to inform policy debates.",
      "reason": "A rationale is given for the publication-year restriction, but other notable restrictions (e.g., excluding coexisting medical conditions, qualitative studies) lack explicit rationales in the eligibility criteria, though the Discussion briefly justifies excluding qualitative studies."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 88
}

